.TH TR 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
tr \- translate, squeeze, or delete characters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tr
.RB [ \-cCds ]
.I string1
.RI [ string2 ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tr
copies standard input to standard output and changes it on the way.
Depending on the options, it translates the bytes of
.I string1
to the corresponding bytes of
.IR string2 ,
deletes the bytes of
.IR string1 ,
or squeezes each run of a repeated byte to a single byte.
It takes no file operands, so redirect or pipe the input.
.PP
By default
.B tr
translates.
Each input byte that is in
.I string1
becomes the byte at the same position in
.IR string2 .
If
.I string2
is shorter, its last byte stands for the rest.
If it is longer, the extra bytes are ignored.
If it expands to nothing, as a backwards range does, the bytes of
.I string1
become the digit
.BR 0 .
If a byte occurs more than once in
.IR string1 ,
its last occurrence counts.
All other bytes are copied unchanged, and so is everything when there is
no
.IR string2 .
.PP
.B tr
works on bytes, not characters.
A character that takes several bytes, such as one in UTF-8, is handled
as separate bytes; see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Strings
Each byte of
.I string1
and
.I string2
stands for itself, except in the constructs below.
.TP
.IB first \- last
Every byte from
.I first
through
.IR last ,
by byte value.
A range with
.I last
below
.I first
is empty, which is not an error.
Either end may be an escape.
A dash at the start or end of a string is an ordinary byte, but a first
operand that starts with one, other than a lone dash, needs
.B \-\-
before it, or it is read as options.
.TP
.B \e
Starts an escape, one of:
.RS
.TP
.BR \ea ", " \eb ", " \ee ", " \ef ", " \en ", " \er ", " \et ", " \ev
The bell (0x07), backspace (0x08), escape (0x1B), form feed (0x0C),
newline (0x0A), carriage return (0x0D), tab (0x09), and vertical tab
(0x0B).
.TP
.B \e\e
A backslash.
.TP
.BI \e ooo
The byte with octal value
.IR ooo ,
from one to three digits, stopping before the value would exceed 255.
.TP
.BI \ex hh
The byte with hexadecimal value
.IR hh ,
one or two digits.
.RE
.IP
Any other backslash, including one at the end of a string, is an
ordinary byte, and the byte after it is read as usual, so
.B \e8
is a backslash and an 8.
A backslash, or an escape for one, before a dash is the exception; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BI [: class :]
Every byte of the class, in ascending order.
The classes are
.BR alnum ,
.BR alpha ,
.BR blank ,
.BR cntrl ,
.BR digit ,
.BR graph ,
.BR lower ,
.BR print ,
.BR punct ,
.BR space ,
.BR upper ,
and
.BR xdigit .
They have their meanings in the C locale: only ASCII bytes are in a
class, and bytes from 0x80 up are in none.
Class names are lowercase.
Anything else after
.B [:
is not a class, and the
.B [
is an ordinary byte.
.TP
.BI [= c =]
The single byte
.IR c ,
taken literally.
Use it for a byte that means something else, such as a dash in the
middle of a string,
.BR [=\-=] ,
or a backslash,
.BR [=\e=] .
A
.B [=
not followed by exactly one byte and
.B =]
makes
.B tr
print its usage message and exit with status 1.
.PP
A bracket that starts neither of those is an ordinary byte, so
.B [a\-z]
is the range
.B a\-z
with a bracket at each end.
The POSIX repeats
.BI [ c * n ]
and
.BI [ c *]
are not supported, and are ordinary bytes too.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-cd .
They must come before the operands: the first operand ends the options,
so a later
.B \-d
is another operand.
.B \-\-
ends the options, and is needed when
.I string1
starts with a dash and is not a lone dash.
There must be one or two operands.
Otherwise
.B tr
prints its usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-C
Use the complement of
.I string1
in its place everywhere: every byte value from 0 to 255 that is not in
it, in ascending order, newline included.
The two options are the same.
.TP
.B \-d
Delete the bytes of
.I string1
instead of translating them.
All other bytes are copied.
A
.I string2
is allowed but translates nothing; it matters only to
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B \-s
Squeeze runs.
After translation and deletion, a run of one repeated output byte is
replaced by a single byte if that byte is in
.IR string2 ,
or in
.I string1
when there is no
.IR string2 .
A deleted byte does not end a run, so
.B tr \-ds b a
turns
.B abab
into
.BR a .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B tr
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
The wildcards are
.BR * " and " ? ,
even when quoted, so a string containing one is replaced by the names
of the files that match it, as described in
.BR busybox (1).
A string that matches no file is left as it is.
To prevent this, write
.B *
as
.B \e052
and
.B ?\&
as
.BR \e077 .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, the wrong number of operands, an empty
.IR string2 ,
a malformed equivalence class, a read or write error, or out of memory.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Change lowercase letters to uppercase:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo hello | tr a\-z A\-Z
HELLO
.EE
.RE
.PP
Do the same with classes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tr \(aq[:lower:]\(aq \(aq[:upper:]\(aq <in.txt >out.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove the CRs from a file with CR LF line endings:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tr \-d \(aq\er\(aq <dos.txt >unix.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Change backslashes in a list of paths to forward slashes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tr \(aq\e\e\(aq / <paths.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the words of a text one per line, by changing each run of
characters that are not letters or digits to a newline:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tr \-cs \(aq[:alnum:]\(aq \(aq\en\(aq <text.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep only printable ASCII characters and newlines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tr \-cd \(aq[:print:]\en\(aq <data.bin >text.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Change underscores and dashes to spaces.
The last byte of
.I string2
fills out the rest, and a dash at the end of a string is literal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tr \(aq_\-\(aq \(aq \(aq <names.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR tr :
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-C
is the same as
.BR \-c .
POSIX has
.B \-C
complement characters and
.B \-c
complement byte values, but here both complement byte values.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-t ,
and there are no long options except
.BR \-\-help .
.IP \(bu 2
These are errors in GNU
.BR tr ,
but not here: a lone
.I string1
without
.B \-d
or
.BR \-s ,
which copies the input; a
.I string2
with
.B \-d
but not
.BR \-s ,
which is ignored; a range that runs backwards, which is empty; and an
unknown class name, which is ordinary text.
.IP \(bu 2
Options are not recognized after the first operand, where GNU
.B tr
still accepts them.
.PP
A backslash before a dash does not work as in GNU
.BR tr .
When a backslash, or an escape for one such as
.B \e\e
or
.BR \e134 ,
comes right before a dash,
.B tr
adds a dash and then reads on from the byte after the first backslash.
So
.B \e\-
is two dashes, not one, which moves the later bytes out of line with the
other string,
.B \e\e\-
is three dashes with no backslash, and a final
.B \e134\-
is the bytes
.BR \-134\- .
Instead, write a dash as
.B \e055
or
.BR [=\-=] ,
or last in the string, and a backslash before a dash as
.BR [=\e=] .
.PP
Strings reach
.B tr
as UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later, and in the system code
page on earlier versions, but
.B tr
works on bytes either way.
In UTF-8, the character e with an acute accent is the bytes 0xC3 0xA9,
and using it as
.I string1
translates each of the two bytes alone.
Ranges and classes cannot select non-ASCII characters.
NUL bytes are ordinary, and
.B \e0
puts one in a string.
.PP
Standard input and output are binary, so CR LF line endings and Ctrl-Z
characters are copied unchanged.
A CR is a byte like any other, and
.B tr\ \-d\ \(aq\er\(aq
removes it.
.PP
Lines typed at a console arrive with CR LF line endings.
To end the input, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and press Enter.
Ctrl-D is an ordinary character.
Text typed at a console is converted from the console's input code page
to the ANSI code page, except when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, when it
stays in the console's code page, so a typed non-ASCII character does
not arrive as UTF-8.
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later.
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
A block of output that contains a NUL byte is written as is, with no
conversion or emulation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dos2unix (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR unix2dos (1)
